    "suggestedTimeMinutes": 40,
    "minWordCount": 250,
    "modelEssay": "In contemporary economic discourse, the rapid proliferation of artificial intelligence has sparked contentious debate regarding its long-term impact on the global workforce. While some commentators argue that automated algorithms will induce catastrophic structural unemployment, others contend that AI will catalyze higher-tier occupations and stimulate economic prosperity. In my view, although transitionary disruption is inevitable, the capacity of AI to generate innovative industries and enrich human productivity far outweighs its displacing effects.\n\nOn the one hand, apprehensions regarding job obsolescence are firmly grounded in observable trends. Unlike previous industrial revolutions that primarily automated manual tasks, cognitive computing and machine learning models now execute intricate diagnostic, administrative, and analytical functions previously reserved for skilled professionals. If commercial enterprises replace substantial cohorts of entry-level knowledge workers with software operating continuously without wage demands, severe socioeconomic dislocation could occur before regulatory and educational frameworks can adapt. For instance, customer support departments and automated legal document discovery have already seen thousands of roles eliminated across multinational corporations.\n\nOn the other hand, historical precedent demonstrates that disruptive innovation consistently reconfigures labor markets rather than destroying aggregate employment. By delegating repetitive computational analysis to intelligent algorithms, human employees are liberated to prioritize strategic conceptualization, complex ethical reasoning, and empathetic interpersonal collaboration—capacities that synthetic systems cannot authenticate. Moreover, an expansive technological ecosystem is flourishing around artificial intelligence, necessitating millions of data governance specialists, algorithm auditors, and systems integrators. The advent of personal computing similarly displaced traditional typists yet created vast software and digital engineering sectors.\n\nIn conclusion, while the progression toward cognitive automation will undoubtedly displace vulnerable occupations in the short term, I believe that proactive workforce retraining and curricular modernization will harness this transformation. Ultimately, artificial intelligence will foster a more dynamic knowledge-driven economy characterized by cognitively sophisticated, high-value employment."
